      "latency_ms": 6741.9,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 645,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and one of the most abundant and essential substances on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical Definition\n* **Formula:** $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$\n* **Composition:** Each molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **State of Matter:** At standard temperature and pressure, water is a transparent, odorless, and tasteless **liquid**. However, it is famous for naturally existing in three distinct states on Earth:\n  * **Liquid** (water in rivers, oceans, and rain)\n  * **Solid** (ice in glaciers, snow, and hail)\n  * **Gas** (water vapor in steam, fog, and clouds)\n\n### 2. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater has several unique and unusual properties that make it vital for life:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure (it is a polar molecule, meaning one side has a slight positive charge and the other a slight negative charge), water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is how it transports nutrients in living things.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of plants).\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat with only small changes in its own temperature. This helps regulate the climate of the Earth and the body temperatures of living organisms.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. This is why ice floats, which prevents lakes and oceans from freezing solid from the bottom up, allowing aquatic life to survive the winter.\n\n### 3. Biological Importance (The Stuff of Life)\n* **The Medium of Life:** Life as we know it is entirely dependent on water. The first living organisms evolved in Earth's oceans, and all living things—from bacteria to humans—are mostly made of water (about 60% of an adult human body is water).\n* **Metabolic Reactions:** Water is not just a passive environment; it actively participates in many chemical reactions inside cells, helping to digest food, eliminate waste, and regulate temperature.\n\n### 4. Ecological and Geographical Importance\n* **Earth's Blanket:** Water covers roughly **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly recycled through the hydrologic cycle: evaporating from the surface, rising into the atmosphere to form clouds, falling back down as precipitation (rain or snow), and flowing back into the oceans via rivers.\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties that acts as the lifeblood of our planet."
